When should you use CQRS?

CQRS is useful when:

Reads and writes have very different requirements
System is read-heavy
Read queries are very complex
You need highly optimized read models
You want to scale read and write independently
You have event-driven architecture



You can scale them separately:

                Application
                /         \
               ↓           ↓
          Write Service   Read Service
               |             |
               ↓             ↓
           Write DB       Read DB
               3 nodes     20 nodes


Interview one-liner

CQRS separates the write model from the read model. The write model is optimized for transactional consistency, 
while the read model is optimized for fast queries and is often denormalized. 
The read model can be updated asynchronously from events, which provides scalability at the cost of eventual consistency.




What if the Consumer Processes the Same Event Twice?

This can happen.

For example:

Consumer receives OrderCreated
      ↓
Updates Read DB
      ↓
Crashes before acknowledging Kafka
      ↓
Kafka sends event again

Now the same event is processed twice.

So the consumer should be idempotent.




